*** THE TRIAGE NOTE'S GRAMMAR WAS WRONG, AND IT MATTERS. *** Triage recorded
`mail smtpserver <ip> authentication <mech>`. The parser does not accept that:

  awplus(config)# mail smtpserver  ?
    A.B.C.D         IPv4 address of SMTP Server
    WORD            Fully Qualified Domain Name of SMTP Server
    X:X::X:X        IPv6 address of SMTP Server
    authentication  Authentication mechanism
    port            SMTP server port
    tls             Use TLS connection to secure sending mail

  awplus(config)# mail smtpserver 10.38.22.33  ?
    <cr>                                  <-- the address takes NOTHING after it
  awplus(config)# mail smtpserver 10.38.22.33 authentication  ?
    % Unrecognized command                <-- NOT a sub-option of the address

`authentication`, `port` and `tls` are SIBLINGS of the address, i.e. three independent config
lines. The full surface, all confirmed by `?`:

  config mode:
    mail smtpserver {A.B.C.D | WORD | X:X::X:X}
    mail smtpserver authentication {crammd5 | login | oauth2 | plain}
        ... crammd5|login|plain  -> `username  Username string` (NO <cr>: username is REQUIRED)
        ... username <USERNAME>  -> `password  Password string`
        ... oauth2               -> `<cr>` (no username, no password)
    mail smtpserver port <1-65535>   ("Specify the port value (default: SMTP(25),
                                       STARTTLS(587), SMTPS(465))")
    mail smtpserver tls {disable-cert-check | smtps | starttls}
    mail from <FROM>
    log email <EMAIL-ADDRESS> [exclude|facility|level|msgtext|program|time]
    no mail smtpserver [authentication | port | tls]   (bare form removes the server)
  exec mode:
    mail to <TO> [subject <SUBJECT>] [file <FILE>]
    show mail [oauth2 url]

The four mechanisms the DUT offers are therefore: crammd5, login, oauth2, plain.

================================================================================
TOOLS USED -- exact arguments
================================================================================
NEW, written for this case, committed to tools/ with their README entries:

  tools/smtpsrv.py -- an SMTP server in pure Python on any TCP port that can REQUIRE
    authentication, advertising any subset of PLAIN / LOGIN / CRAM-MD5, logging a timestamped
    C:/S: transcript of every line in both directions. This is the instrument the case is
    graded on: the DUT is the client, so the server side IS the wire.
    Why it had to be written: the box's Postfix advertises no AUTH at all, and adding SASL to
    it -- or binding port 25 -- is root (STANDING-ORDERS §4), which an unattended window
    cannot ask for. `mail smtpserver port <1-65535>` is configurable, so an unprivileged
    listener needs NO root at all.
    Validated OFFLINE before it touched the bench: driven with python3 smtplib through all
    three mechanisms, right credential and wrong, with --require-auth; 3 AUTH-OK + 3
    AUTH-REJECTED 535 + 3 MAIL FROM refused at 530 + 3 messages stored.
    On the box:
      tools/tb tool tb277 smtp1304 smtpsrv --port 11025 --bind 0.0.0.0 --hostname ck-smtp \
        --user ckmail:<pw> [--mech none] [--require-auth] --maildir /tmp/smtp1304/mail-<W> \
        --log /tmp/smtp1304/srv-<W>.log --stopfile /tmp/smtp1304/stop<W> --seconds N

  tools/conkey.py -- sends raw keys/lines to a console that is NOT at a prompt, with no login.
    Why it had to be written: `mail to <addr> subject <s>` is INTERACTIVE -- it answers "Type
    the message below and finish with a CTRL-d" and then eats every following line as message
    body. No prompt-driven tool here can drive that, and when ckcon hit it, it hung for 90 s
    and then PARKED THE CONSOLE in body-entry mode, where even ckcon's own login could not
    reach it ("no privileged prompt on /dev/u2"). peek.py can report such a console but can
    only send a bare CR. conkey is the counterpart: peek reports the state, conkey changes it.
    It also covers gate-item-2 recovery generally.
    Registered as a tb console action (one line in tools/tb's CON_TBT), so it gets the
    console-holder refusal and the /tmp/<scratch>/console-<uN>.log transcript convention.
      tools/tb conkey tb277 smtp1304 u2 115200 wait:2 'line:mail to ... subject ...' \
        wait:2 'line:<body>' key:ctrl-d wait:12 --final 6

EXISTING tools used unchanged:
  tools/tb precheck|scratch|template|probe|peek|qmark|ckcon|health|tool|capture|put|tail|grep|ps
  tools/tcpflow.py  -- via: tools/tb tool tb277 smtp1304 tcpflow /tmp/smtp1304/e-postfix.pcap
                       --port 25 --ascii   (decoded the independent tcpdump of the Postfix leg)
  tools/tb capture tb277 smtp1304 eth3 45 e-postfix.pcap tcp port 25 and host 10.38.22.34

A NOTE ON THE PASSWORD. `ckSmtp1` is a throwaway credential invented for this case's own
listener. It authenticates nothing that exists outside this case, it was created at 17:08 NZDT
and removed at 17:20 NZDT, and it is not a bench, device or account secret. Sending it down the
