             Authentication : crammd5   <-- STILL crammd5
  awplus# show running-config
             mail smtpserver authentication crammd5 username ckmail password 8 fbq5A0iO...
                                           ^^^^^^^ unchanged
  On the wire (srv-A.log connection 6), the trigger that followed sent:
             04:11:03Z C: AUTH CRAM-MD5     <-- still CRAM-MD5. Not oauth2, not nothing.
  Reproduced immediately: 17:11:49 the same command again, running-config still crammd5.

A7, from a CLEAN state (after `no mail smtpserver authentication`, nothing configured):
  17:13:03 awplus(config)# mail smtpserver authentication oauth2    <-- again accepted silently
  awplus# show running-config -> contains `mail smtpserver 10.38.22.33` and
          `mail smtpserver port 11025` and NO `authentication` line at all
  awplus# show mail           -> NO Authentication field, NO Username field
  => it is not "oauth2 cannot replace an existing mechanism". The command does NOTHING, EVER.

The device contradicts itself, in its own words:
  awplus# show mail oauth2 url
  % The smtpserver is not configured for OAUTH2
...read immediately after the CLI had accepted `mail smtpserver authentication oauth2` without
complaint.

WHY THIS IS A FINDING and not an unsupported feature:
  * the parser OFFERS it -- `mail smtpserver authentication ?` lists `oauth2  OAUTH2
    authentication`, and `mail smtpserver authentication oauth2 ?` offers `<cr>`, i.e. it is a
    complete and valid command, not a truncated one;
  * it is ACCEPTED with no diagnostic whatsoever;
  * it has NO effect on running-config, on `show mail`, or on the wire;
  * and there is a security consequence. An operator who sets `oauth2` believes the device has
    moved to token-based authentication. It has not: it silently keeps sending the PASSWORD --
    in a CRAM-MD5 digest here, and in CLEARTEXT base64 had the previous setting been `plain`.
    The operator gets no signal at all that the change did not take.
  * `mail oauth2` and `show mail oauth2 url` exist, so the feature is partly built. Whether
    oauth2 needs a prior authorisation step is beside the point: the command should then be
    REJECTED with a diagnostic, not accepted silently.

*** THIS IS A FIFTH SILENT DISCARD ON THIS PLATFORM, AND IT IS A NEW SHAPE. *** Groups A and B
found four, all of them a WEAKENING in which a setting was silently replaced by a weaker one.
This one is different: an accepted command that does nothing at all, leaving the PREVIOUS
setting silently in force. Same root symptom -- configuration accepted, configuration
discarded, operator told nothing.
STEP VERDICT: UNSUPPORTED (this limb), and the DEFECT is the SILENCE, not the absence.
  Why UNSUPPORTED and not FAIL: on the evidence, oauth2 is a partially-built feature on this
  build -- the CLI token exists, the function does not, and the device says so itself
  ("% The smtpserver is not configured for OAUTH2"). STANDING-ORDERS §1: a missing feature is
  UNSUPPORTED. What is NOT acceptable, and is the finding, is that an unsupported mechanism is
  OFFERED by the parser and ACCEPTED with no diagnostic instead of being rejected.
  🔴 SCOPE LIMIT, stated plainly: this bench CANNOT exercise oauth2 end-to-end at all -- there
  is no OAuth2 provider, no token and no `mail oauth2` authorisation anywhere on tb277. So this
  case does NOT establish whether oauth2 authentication would work given a token. What it does
  establish, and what needs no provider, is that the CONFIG COMMAND is inert: running-config
  never changes. Reproduced three times.

