================================================================================
STEP C1 -- SERVER OFFERS NO AUTH: does the DUT silently downgrade? (NO)
================================================================================
The security question this case most needs answered: with `authentication login` configured,
what does the DUT do against a server that advertises no AUTH? If it sent the mail anyway that
would be a silent downgrade.
Server: smtpsrv --mech none (no AUTH line in EHLO at all), and deliberately NO --require-auth,
so that a downgrade WOULD have succeeded and been plainly visible.

Server (srv-C.log, all 4 connections identical):
  04:16:44Z C: EHLO ck.invalid
  04:16:44Z S: 250-ck-smtp / 250-PIPELINING / 250-SIZE 10240000 / 250 HELP   (no AUTH line)
  04:16:44Z C: QUIT
  04:16:44Z S: 221 2.0.0 Bye
  04:17:06Z SUMMARY connections=4 ehlo=4 mail_from=0 mail_from_denied=0 messages=0 quit=4
  04:17:06Z SUMMARY auth: NO AUTH verb was ever sent by any client
DUT console: msmtp: the server does not support authentication
             msmtp: could not send mail (account default from /etc/msmtprc)
RESULT: the DUT read the EHLO, saw no AUTH, and QUIT IMMEDIATELY. No credential offered, no
MAIL FROM, no message body -- mail_from=0, messages=0, across four attempts. The mail stayed
queued rather than being sent in the clear. **No silent downgrade.** Correct, secure behaviour,
and reported as a positive finding.
STEP VERDICT: PASS.

