  A2   authentication plain,    correct credential    AUTH PLAIN    -> 235, delivered  PASS
  A3   authentication login,    correct credential    AUTH LOGIN    -> 235, delivered  PASS
  A4   authentication crammd5,  correct credential    AUTH CRAM-MD5 -> 235, delivered  PASS
  A5   authentication oauth2, replacing crammd5       accepted, NO effect       UNSUPPORTED
  A7   authentication oauth2, from a clean state      accepted, NO effect       UNSUPPORTED
  --   retention: tls / port vs authentication        all three independent, retained  PASS
  A6   no mail smtpserver authentication (removal)    AUTH verb STOPS, refused 530     PASS
  B1   crammd5, wrong credential (control)            digest mismatch -> 535           PASS
  B2   plain,   wrong credential (control)            535, no MAIL FROM                PASS
  B3   login,   wrong credential (control)            535, no MAIL FROM                PASS
  C1   server offers no AUTH (control)                EHLO/QUIT, no downgrade          PASS
  E1   real Postfix :25, no AUTH (control, pcap)      EHLO/QUIT, 23 B, no downgrade    PASS
  12 of 14 legs PASS, 2 UNSUPPORTED (the same oauth2 limb, measured from two states).
  NOTHING IN THIS CASE WENT UNMEASURED -- there is no NOT RUN step and nothing to unblock.

FINDINGS
  1. *** PRODUCT DEFECT (reported as a finding, not as the case verdict) *** `mail smtpserver
     authentication oauth2` is accepted by the
     CLI with no diagnostic and has no effect at all -- running-config, `show mail` and the
     wire are all unchanged, and `show mail oauth2 url` then answers "% The smtpserver is not
     configured for OAUTH2". The previous mechanism silently stays in force, so an operator who
     believes they have moved to token authentication is still sending the password.
     A FIFTH silent discard on this platform, and a new shape: not a weakening, a no-op.
     Reproduced 3 times, from both a configured and a clean state.
  2. POSITIVE: no silent downgrade. With authentication configured and a server that offers no
     AUTH, the DUT sends EHLO then QUIT -- it does not fall back to sending unauthenticated.
     Confirmed against our instrument AND against a real Postfix, the latter with a packet
     capture taken outside the instrument.
  3. POSITIVE: the password is stored ENCRYPTED (type 8) in running-config and is not shown at
     all by `show mail`.
  4. POSITIVE: CRAM-MD5 is genuinely implemented -- a real HMAC-MD5 over the server's random
     challenge, verified by the server, with the password never on the wire.
  5. NEGATIVE RESULT (reported as such): `authentication`, `port` and `tls` do NOT displace one
     another. No silent discard in this subsystem.
  6. PRODUCT INTERNALS: the DUT's SMTP client is msmtp, driven from /etc/msmtprc, and it
     reports the server's reply text to the operator verbatim. A refused message is queued and
     retried on the next send.
  7. DOCUMENTATION / TRIAGE CORRECTION: the grammar is `mail smtpserver authentication <mech>
     username <u> password <p>` as a line in its own right, NOT
     `mail smtpserver <ip> authentication <mech>` as triage recorded. username/password are
     mandatory for crammd5/login/plain and absent for oauth2.

VERDICT BLOCK
  FINDING: `mail smtpserver authentication oauth2` is offered by the parser, accepted with no
    diagnostic, and never applied -- running-config, `show mail` and the wire all keep the
    PREVIOUS mechanism, and `show mail oauth2 url` then answers "% The smtpserver is not
    configured for OAUTH2". A fifth silent discard on this platform and a new shape: not a
    weakening, a no-op. Security consequence: an operator who believes they moved to token
    authentication is still sending the password. Reproduced 3 times.
CAVEAT: three of the four advertised mechanisms (plain, login, crammd5) are proven end-to-end on
  the wire. The fourth, oauth2, is NOT proven either way: tb277 has no OAuth2 provider, no token
  and no `mail oauth2` authorisation, so this run could not exercise oauth2 authentication
  itself. What it did establish, and what needs no provider, is that the oauth2 CONFIG COMMAND
  is inert. Whether oauth2 would authenticate given a token is NOT MEASURABLE on this bench.
  Also not covered: TLS-wrapped delivery (`mail smtpserver tls smtps|starttls`) was set and
  removed as a config-retention check only, never exercised against a TLS listener; and the
  `log email` trigger path was not used -- every send was driven by the deterministic exec
  `mail to ... subject ...` trigger.

VERDICT: PASS -- SMTP server authentication works and is proven on the wire for all three real mechanisms (plain, login, crammd5), each with correct-credential acceptance at 235, wrong-credential rejection at 535, an OFF baseline that sends no AUTH verb at all, a no-downgrade control against two independent servers, and removal that stops the AUTH verb; the fourth advertised mechanism, oauth2, is a non-functional limb on this build (UNSUPPORTED) whose silent acceptance is recorded as a product finding, not as a failure of the feature under test.
